#
# prima-materia — the Ashtar transmission, entered at Van Tassel's I Rode a Flying Saucer!
#
# Opened from the structure-driven entrance of SPEC §14: registered at
# pm:corpusNamed in traditions/registry-channeled.ttl with no node. The work
# opened is the first printed collection of the messages, read whole.
#
# Order of the file: the works; the tradition with the three decisions; the
# receivings, one per claimed speaker; the namings; the practices; the
# capacities; the yieldings; the chains; the cautions; the conditions of
# failure; the signs of success; the protocol.
#
# Identifiers: AshtarTransmission plus the name as the edition spells it, or
# plus a short English term for what the work describes, since the work is
# English.
#

pmw:AshtarTransmissionIRodeAFlyingSaucer a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "I Rode a Flying Saucer! The Mystery of the Flying Saucers Revealed"@en, "I Rode a Flying Saucer! The Mystery of the Flying Saucers Revealed"@de ;
    skos:note "George W. Van Tassel, second edition, revised and enlarged; the title page of the text layer names New Age Publishing Co., Glendale Boulevard, Los Angeles, and copyright 1952 by Van Tassel. The body carries dated messages from 6 January 1952 to 20 March 1953, the later ones under a heading saying they arrived after the first edition was printed, so this printing cannot be earlier than spring 1953. Read whole, author's note to conclusion, from the Internet Archive full text of the identifier i-rode-a-flying-saucer-the-mystery-of-the-flying-saucers-revealed-george-w-van-tassel-z-lib.org (66052 characters, publisher's advertisements after p. 51). Page numbers are taken from the running folios in the text layer; p. 44 has no folio there and is placed between 43 and 45."@en ;
    skos:note "A second carrier, the identifier 1952-i-rode-a-flying-saucer-by-george-w.-van-tassel (also under the suffix _202407), is a later printing of the same second edition: its title page drops the publisher's address, and it adds after p. 51 an annotation signed G. W. Van Tassel, Box 419, Yucca Valley, dated 1 September 1955, naming him head of his College of Universal Wisdom and announcing Into This World and Out Again. The 54 messages under 52 dated headings, their order and wording agree between the two carriers wherever compared. The front matter does not: the New Age printing opens with a publisher's note signed Franklin Thomas, which glosses Shan, Blaau, Schare and ventlas and describes the state of reception, while the later printing replaces it with an author's note that concedes errors in the receptions. A further upload, documents.pub_i-rode-a-flying-saucer-george-van-tassel, carries the same PDF as the first. The first edition (1952), which ended at the message of 3 October 1952, was not found."@en ;
    skos:note "The work is in English; the rule of two translations has no object here. The two carriers stand in for it as two printings."@en .

pmw:AshtarTransmissionCouncilOfSevenLights a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Council of Seven Lights"@en, "The Council of Seven Lights"@de ;
    skos:note "George W. Van Tassel, The Council of Seven Lights (Los Angeles: DeVorss & Co., 1958), copyright the Ministry of Universal Wisdom, Inc., Yucca Valley, per the title page of the text layer. Not opened in this run beyond the title page and contents, ten chapters from The Missing Link to The False Chariot. Reachable whole at the Internet Archive under 33979370-the-council-of-seven-lights-1958-by-george-van-tassel, text layer 198052 characters, ASCII letter share 1.0, not access-restricted. I Rode a Flying Saucer! announces it twice as the place where Uni and Shanchea are explained. The next work to open for this tradition."@en .

pmw:AshtarTransmissionProceedingsCollegeOfUniversalWisdom a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Proceedings of the College of Universal Wisdom"@en, "Proceedings of the College of Universal Wisdom"@de ;
    skos:note "The periodical of Van Tassel's College of Universal Wisdom, Joshua Tree and later Yucca Valley, from 1953. Not opened in this run. A compilation of issues lies at the Internet Archive under universal-songs-of-freedom, titled Universal Wisdom: Identified and Acknowledged, with George W. Van Tassel and Daniel R. Hollingsworth as creators; its text layer of 326474 characters begins with Volume 1, Number 2, 1 November 1953, which lists the Seven Lights council on Shanchea, the twelve lords on Uni, and Ashtar under a higher commandant of the Vela sector. The layer is in part hand-lettered and typewritten, and the first pages read in fragments; whether all issues are carried, and how many are legible, was not measured. An audio item PROCEEDINGSOFTHECOLLEGEOFUNIVERSALWISDOMVol111GeorgeVanTassel carries no text."@en .

pmw:AshtarTransmissionIntoThisWorldAndOutAgain a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Into This World and Out Again"@en, "Into This World and Out Again"@de ;
    skos:note "George W. Van Tassel, announced in the annotation of 1955 in the later printing of I Rode a Flying Saucer! as a new book of evidence gathered over four years. A title search at the Internet Archive on 2026-10-29 returned no item. Not tried: other digitisation projects, the publisher's catalogues, a search under the author with other title words."@en .

pmw:AshtarTransmissionWhenStarsLookDown a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "When Stars Look Down"@en, "When Stars Look Down"@de ;
    skos:note "George W. Van Tassel, When Stars Look Down (Los Angeles: The Kruckeberg Press, 1976), whose introduction calls it part of his writings compiled over twenty years. Not opened in this run beyond the title page and introduction. Reachable at the Internet Archive under george-van-tassel-when-stars-look-down-1976 (text layer 424215 characters) and under when-stars-look-down-george-van-tassel, an upload described as cleaned (456099 characters); neither is access-restricted."@en .

pmw:AshtarTransmissionMensagensCosmicasAshtar a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Mensagens Cósmicas Ashtar"@pt, "Mensagens Cósmicas Ashtar"@en ;
    skos:note "Paulo Antonio L. Fernandes, Mensagens Cósmicas Ashtar, an edition of the Centro de Estudos Exobiológicos Ashtar Sheran, which its back matter says Fernandes founded in 1973 in Brazil; he died in 1981, and the closing messages are dated 1988 and ascribed to other receivers. A published Ashtar message collection of the Brazilian line under the form Ashtar Sheran, outside the Van Tassel line. Not opened in this run beyond the first page of the text layer; reachable at the Internet Archive under 00-33-mensagens-co-smicas-ashtar (text layer 159546 characters, Portuguese)."@en .

pmw:AshtarTransmissionTuellaProjectWorldEvacuation a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Project: World Evacuation"@en, "Project: World Evacuation"@de ;
    skos:note "Tuella (Thelma B. Terrell), Project: World Evacuation, the best-known later collection of messages under the Ashtar Command name, named here from general knowledge of the corpus and not from a read title page. A search at the Internet Archive on 2026-10-29 for the title phrase and for the pen name returned no item. Not tried: other digitisation projects and other title forms."@en .

pmt:AshtarTransmission a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AshtarTransmissionIRodeAFlyingSaucer ;
    rdfs:label "Ashtar transmission"@en, "Ashtar-Überlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of messages ascribed to a command or council of non-terrestrial beings charged with the oversight of the earth, received after the first by many transmitters who do not share a lineage."@en ;
    skos:note "The motif of a federation or confederation of worlds enters general circulation here and is afterwards found in corpora that never name Ashtar. That spread is a reception history and belongs in the graph as one, before anybody reads it as agreement between independent witnesses."@en ;
    skos:note "Raised from pm:corpusNamed on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: the whole of I Rode a Flying Saucer!, second edition, in two printings. What was not read: The Council of Seven Lights, the Proceedings of the College of Universal Wisdom, When Stars Look Down, and every later collection outside Van Tassel; each stands at its work node with the reason."@en ;
    skos:note "The first of the three decisions SPEC §14 asks for, the contact route, stays at documented, and the route is printed in the work. Van Tassel sends the reader of the October 1952 message to page 604 of Oahspe for the meaning of an arrow, so the received Spiritualist literature lay open on his table; the messages carry the vocabulary of light, vibration and the Creator that the earlier received corpora had circulated; and the conclusion disclaims others who had published material from the meetings under other titles, which shows the messages spreading beyond the receiver within the first year. The Ra material, read earlier for pmt:RaMaterial, names Van Tassel and says his positive contact was lost (pmc:RaGeorgeVanTasselNaming), so that later corpus knew this one. Agreement of this work with any channelled corpus in the holding is reception along that route and counts once."@en ;
    skos:note "The second decision, recension and edition: the second edition, revised and enlarged, of 1952, in the printing with the publisher's imprint, checked against a later printing of the same edition that adds an annotation of 1955. The messages agree between them; the front matter differs, a publisher's note in the first and an author's note conceding errors in the second, and both are entered with the printing named. The first edition, which ended in October 1952, was not reached; the later messages, from 17 October 1952, are the additions of the second."@en ;
    skos:note "The third decision, the number of citable places: 54 messages under 52 dated headings from 33 named speakers, and one undated reception from an unnamed speaker, pp. 18-48, framed by the author's note, an introduction, an opening essay, commentary after many messages and a conclusion, about 60000 characters, cited by page and message date. The messages are short, many are greetings with a rank and a position in space, and the operative claims lie in three places: the receiver's account of how he learned to receive (pp. 15-17), the rays and beams said to be sent to the group (pp. 29-30 and 37), and the warnings and forecasts."@en ;
    skos:note "What the harvest found, in counts. Fifteen yieldings naming nineteen capacities said to be obtainable by practice, most of them capacities of reception or perception; four chains, one a stated necessity; eleven cautions, two of them warnings against the receptions themselves, one by the publisher and one, in the later printing only, by the author; twenty-six conditions of failure, twenty-five of them forecasts about the world with a public outcome, most of them dated or with a term, and one a null outcome for a rival practice; and twelve signs of success, three bodily or visual signs in the group and nine fulfilments the author or a speaker claims for a forecast. Against the signs stands one fulfilment the author himself says has not come (pmc:AshtarTransmissionLeektowPrediction) and the author's own warning that events will show errors in the receptions (pmc:AshtarTransmissionReceptionErrorsCaution)."@en ;
    skos:note "Open, and the next place to go: The Council of Seven Lights, reachable whole; then the Proceedings of the College of Universal Wisdom, whose legibility is unmeasured; and a collection outside the Van Tassel line, of which Fernandes is reachable. Not entered from this work: the powers the speakers claim for themselves and their ships, which the work does not offer as attainable by practice; they stand in the notes of the namings."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Van Tassel, the Ministry of Universal Wisdom publications; the published Ashtar message collections" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:AshtarTransmissionIRodeAFlyingSaucer, pmw:AshtarTransmissionCouncilOfSevenLights, pmw:AshtarTransmissionProceedingsCollegeOfUniversalWisdom, pmw:AshtarTransmissionIntoThisWorldAndOutAgain, pmw:AshtarTransmissionWhenStarsLookDown, pmw:AshtarTransmissionMensagensCosmicasAshtar, pmw:AshtarTransmissionTuellaProjectWorldEvacuation ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "George W. Van Tassel, I Rode a Flying Saucer! The Mystery of the Flying Saucers Revealed, second edition, revised and enlarged (Los Angeles: New Age Publishing Co., 1952), author's note to p. 51; read in the full-text layer of that edition" .

#
# The receivings, one per claimed speaker
#

pmc:AshtarTransmissionLutbunnReceiving a pm:Receiving ;
    rdfs:label "The work's claim that the messages of 6 January and 8 February 1952 are from Lutbunn"@en, "Die Behauptung des Werkes, die Botschaften vom 6 January and 8 February 1952 stammen von Lutbunn"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work prints under the dates 6 January and 8 February 1952 a message signed by Lutbunn, who says the receiver's contact is held aboard at a stated altitude and returned, that the press will report more sightings, and that his patrol has been told to contact the group whenever it sits; sends power from the Center."@en ;
    skos:note "This node records the claim, not its truth. The citation names Van Tassel as author because that is what a reader can obtain; the title page itself says the book was radioed by other-world intelligences through him."@en ;
    pm:receivedBy "George W. Van Tassel, who says the messages came to him in the group's sessions at Giant Rock and that he is the contact for this reception" ;
    pm:claimedSpeaker "Lutbunn, who names himself a senior commander of the first wave of a planet patrol of Schare's realms" ;
    pm:receptionCircumstance "By the account on pp. 16-17, Van Tassel learned to tune in to vibrations by long study of his own thought, then gathered a small group at regular scheduled meetings to add power to the reception; the message is spoken aloud through his physical body, which he likens to a loudspeaker, and taken down in shorthand, and he says it is given in words humans can understand. The publisher's note of the New Age printing, signed Franklin Thomas, adds that he receives in attunement with the frequency of the communicating intelligence, mostly while his body senses involuntarily, in a condition in which he does not know his hearers are there, sometimes with partial awareness of them. No record of the sessions other than the printed text is mentioned. Dated 6 January and 8 February 1952. The first three messages are said by the author to have served to get used to a powerful vibration." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AshtarTransmission ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AshtarTransmissionIRodeAFlyingSaucer ;
    dcterms:source "George W. Van Tassel, I Rode a Flying Saucer! The Mystery of the Flying Saucers Revealed, second edition, revised and enlarged (Los Angeles: New Age Publishing Co., 1952), p. 18, messages of 6 January and 8 February 1952; read in the full-text layer of that edition" .

