#
# The protocol
#

pmc:AshtarTransmissionHydrogenTest a pm:Testing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AshtarTransmissionIRodeAFlyingSaucer ;
    rdfs:label "Life after the explosion of hydrogen"@en, "Leben nach der Sprengung von Wasserstoff"@de ;
    skos:definition "The central claim of the work, and the one with an outcome no reader can miss: that exploding hydrogen will put out life on the earth, or at least send flames across parts of it for a moment."@en ;
    skos:note "What weakens it is the work's own handling. Three days after newspapers announced a hydrogen bomb in November 1952, Ashtar calls the report partly false and says an isotope does not count as an atomic element, and the author records physicists saying the device was not the hydrogen bomb. A protocol must therefore fix in advance what counts as an explosion of the hydrogen element in the sense of the messages, or every later thermonuclear test can be read as not that. The author's note, that later events would show the receptions to contain errors, gives a second exit."@en ;
    skos:note "No case series is entered. The public record of thermonuclear tests from November 1952 on is the obvious case material and has not been read as a work for this protocol; it would enter at pm:evidenceFrom with its counter-search."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:AshtarTransmissionHydrogenCaution ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "An explosion in which hydrogen is fused, in or near the earth's atmosphere, after which life on the planet continues and no flames sweep over parts of it." ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-10-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:source "George W. Van Tassel, I Rode a Flying Saucer! The Mystery of the Flying Saucers Revealed, second edition, revised and enlarged (Los Angeles: New Age Publishing Co., 1952), pp. 30-32, 36-37 and 44, messages of 18 July, 15 August and 14 November 1952; read in the full-text layer of that edition" .
